#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "692dbdea-922d-568c-bc00-4972dd733ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6305" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3688/Source/Images/ae378f2d7290f1c5b6d5c6b3f26dd450.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x+y^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t) + (2\\sin t)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(2x + y^{2})ds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}4(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t) \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 8∫_{π/2}^{π}(\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "3fe61e6a-ce68-5f85-98f3-e4af5a39de5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9438" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (\\cos t)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} t - 1dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "a4c10c26-84c5-5ec7-9468-93986e27f492" :question/math-academy-id "e-9462" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t)^{2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\cos^{2} t - 2 \\\\\n&= 2(2\\cos^{2} t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}2\\cos 2t \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t$,\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt &= 2∫_{0}^{4π}\\cos udu \\\\\n&= 2\\sin u \\mid _{0}^{4π} \\\\\n&= 2(0 - 0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e96ff12c-2c07-5226-bcac-0a5b1d125519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177329} {:answer/id #uuid "030bb83b-fb03-5eb2-a457-86fde49820ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177330} {:answer/id #uuid "1f658fd4-210e-50bf-8fe1-0728d8f5028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177331} {:answer/id #uuid "33e7b421-1159-5086-9ca7-12c054916050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177332} {:answer/id #uuid "47ef803b-ccc2-57cc-bb4f-1ceafaef8c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f658fd4-210e-50bf-8fe1-0728d8f5028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177331} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e70b79a-0ae0-5af2-9719-027724a6cf99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177328}] :question/id #uuid "b79282b0-117d-50c1-87eb-8d6109e91a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122136" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24c262-4398-5446-bc9d-27645b4f0cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177341} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c38c61-4b53-5b3b-9117-947ce6775169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177342} {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177343} {:answer/id #uuid "0eaf656d-f304-5384-9efa-03f1f9909af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177344} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce387ae-f4c2-5ae9-a54a-6936b3b4fe44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t + 2\\sin t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177343} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f90318c-819f-5544-b17a-bb82ed972697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177340}] :question/id #uuid "c8f5c163-ed26-54b5-8aab-82f3a498034d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122263" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x + y^{2}$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9623430a-8496-5359-946c-836d1db59540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178560} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5de4cb-c5af-53b2-bd49-da848c222d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178561} {:answer/id #uuid "55e07cd7-b8e6-5330-9f9f-8abdbb1229a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178562} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec0d12c-d0d8-58e9-8af9-0b9451a33bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178563} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca3d6fe-fbc3-5944-b4d8-4e57f0f687ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2}} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ca3d6fe-fbc3-5944-b4d8-4e57f0f687ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2}} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178564} :answer-field/id #uuid "665543e2-02cf-50c4-8def-352da6219351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178559}] :question/id #uuid "ce49b8d5-a7c7-525b-89a9-21901c00319a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40255" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3688/Source/Images/q-40255.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$, traversed from the point $(-2, 0)$ to the point $(0,-2)$, as shown above."}]}
